Divert workbench “state” from IndexedDB to server-backed storage (client-side)

Overview

    This patch enables code-server to persist VS Code web workbench state (global/profile/workspace storage) on the server instead of relying on browser IndexedDB.
    A server-backed storage database implementation is introduced in the web layer and conditionally selected through a feature flag carried in the Workbench construction options.
    Workspace uniqueness semantics from unique-db (pathname hashing) are preserved so state remains isolated across different served paths (/ vs /vscode).
    Migration logic is included to bootstrap server storage from existing IndexedDB state; the current version performs migration automatically and logs the action (notification UI with export link can be layered on later).

Details

    Workbench option to enable server-backed state

    The Workbench API surface is extended with an optional boolean flag:
        serverStateStorage?: boolean
    This allows the server to instruct the browser to use a server-backed storage adapter for state persistence instead of IndexedDB.
    Reference: web.api.ts

    Server-backed storage adapter (web)

    A new storage database adapter is added inside the workbench web storage service implementation file. This adapter implements the same storage contract used by the workbench’s Storage layer and is selected only when the feature flag is enabled.
    Core behavior:
        Startup GET: loads all key/values for the current bucket and initializes an in-memory map for fast reads.
        Batched writes: updates are merged locally (insert/delete) and POSTed after a short debounce to reduce I/O and improve responsiveness.
        Live synchronization: a WebSocket subscription listens for external updates on the current bucket and triggers a refresh to keep the in-memory map in sync across tabs/sessions.
        Revision tracking: each successful batch updates a revision value returned by the server, enabling last-writer-wins semantics with resync when required.
        Resilience:
            Pending updates are queued and retried with a small delay on transient failures.
            On WS disconnect, the client automatically attempts to reconnect.
    Reference: storageService.ts

    Selection and wiring in the workbench storage service

    The storage service wiring is updated to choose between the server-backed adapter and the existing IndexedDB-backed adapter based on the serverStateStorage option:
        Application/global scope storage
        Profile storage (non-default profile; default profile aliases to application/global as today)
        Workspace storage
    The implementation preserves workspace uniqueness semantics introduced by unique-db by appending a hash of location.pathname to the workspace bucket identifier. This ensures workspaces served from different paths do not collide when using server-backed storage (and aligns with the behavior of the IndexedDB naming logic).
    Reference: storageService.ts

    Migration from IndexedDB (client-initiated)

    On first initialization of a bucket (global/profile/workspace), if the server responds with an empty dataset but a corresponding IndexedDB dataset exists, a client-side one-time migration occurs:
        IndexedDB items are read and sent to the server as a single batch to initialize server storage for that bucket.
        After migration, the bucket reads from the server only.
    Current version logs the migration and proceeds automatically. A future improvement can show a notification with:
        an export link (download JSON of the IndexedDB items), and
        a confirmation step prior to migration and pruning.
    Reference: storageService.ts

    Cross-tab and multi-session synchronization

    The adapter uses a WebSocket channel to receive change notifications (bucket + changed keys + new revision). Upon receiving a change event, the adapter refreshes the data, updates the in-memory map, and emits the required workbench storage change events.
    BroadcastChannel remains available in the IndexedDB path; the server-backed path relies on the centralized WebSocket updates and in-memory refresh.

    Compatibility and behavior

    The storage adapter follows the same higher-level storage semantics the workbench expects:
        Storage items are strings (objects are serialized at the Storage layer).
        Changed items and deleted keys are surfaced via the standard events used by the workbench.
        optimize() is a no-op for the web adapter (server may compact on demand or via an explicit endpoint).
    The selection logic is non-invasive; when serverStateStorage is false or absent, the code path remains identical to upstream and uses browser IndexedDB as before.

    Security and privacy

    All HTTP/WS requests use same-origin, include credentials, and reuse the existing code-server session. No extra tokens or headers are introduced on the client.

    Testing guide (client)

    Enable serverStateStorage via the server-provided Workbench options and reload.
    Verify:
        Initial load: state is fetched from the server, then kept in-memory.
        Writes: key changes appear on the server via batched POST; subsequent reads reflect changes immediately from memory.
        Multi-tab: open two tabs for the same path; changes from one tab reflect in the other tab shortly after (via WS refresh).
        Workspace uniqueness: Open the same workspace via two different path prefixes (/ and /vscode) and confirm that state does not overlap.
        Migration: Pre-seed IndexedDB with items (using the upstream code path), enable serverStateStorage, reload, and confirm the migration populates server state and the client switches to server reads.

Notes and future improvements

    Notification/Export UX: The current migration path runs automatically with logging. To fully match the planned UX, add a workbench notification offering to export existing IndexedDB data before migration, then proceed with server initialization; prune the IndexedDB copy only after a successful server ACK.
    BroadcastChannel mirroring: As an optimization for same-path tabs, changes received from WS could optionally be echoed into a BroadcastChannel to minimize full refresh cycles (currently not strictly necessary given the centralized WS model).
    Throttling and backoff: the current debounce is tuned for low-latency writes; consider exposing this as a constant or adjusting based on telemetry if needed.

Summary

    This patch equips the web workbench with a robust, server-backed storage path that can be toggled via a Workbench option.
    It preserves existing workspace uniqueness semantics, supports multi-tab and multi-session synchronization, and includes a migration path from IndexedDB.
    When the option is disabled, the web workbench continues to use IndexedDB exactly as before.

